          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During cardiopulmonary bypass, a surgeon needs to pass a clamp behind the aorta </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During cardiopulmonary bypass, a surgeon needs to pass a clamp behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk. Through which pericardial recess can they access these vessels from behind?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oblique sinus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The oblique sinus is a dead-end recess behind the left atrium. It is not a through-passage and cannot provide access behind the great vessels.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Transverse sinus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The transverse sinus runs behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk, forming a passage that allows a surgeon to pass a finger or clamp behind these vessels during bypass surgery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pericardial cavity</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The pericardial cavity is the entire fluid-filled space between the parietal and visceral layers of serous pericardium, not a discrete passage behind the great vessels.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coronary sinus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The coronary sinus is a venous channel that drains deoxygenated blood from the heart wall into the right atrium. It is not a pericardial recess.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Two sinuses lie within the pericardial cavity: the transverse sinus is a passage behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk, while the oblique sinus is a dead-end behind the left atrium. The transverse sinus is the one with surgical importance."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The transverse sinus is the key surgical landmark — a passage behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk used during cardiopulmonary bypass."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
