          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with an anterior myocardial infarction — the pattern sometime</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with an anterior myocardial infarction — the pattern sometimes called the 'widow-maker'. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Right coronary artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>RCA occlusion typically causes an inferior MI, not anterior. The RCA supplies the inferior wall and, in most people, the AV node.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Left circumflex artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>LCx occlusion causes a lateral or posterior MI. The left circumflex supplies the lateral and posterior walls of the left ventricle.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Left anterior descending artery</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The LAD supplies the anterior wall and the interventricular septum. Its occlusion causes an anterior MI — the most dangerous pattern — because of the large myocardial territory it supplies.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Posterior descending artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The PDA is usually a branch of the RCA and supplies the inferior wall. Its territory corresponds to inferior MI, not anterior.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Each coronary artery supplies a specific territory: LAD → anterior wall, RCA → inferior wall, LCx → lateral and posterior wall. Matching the artery to its territory identifies the MI pattern."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The LAD is the most commonly blocked artery in heart attacks, and anterior MI carries the highest mortality."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
